<header><p class="st-kicker">prince bishopric · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Imperial Abbey of Zwiefalten</h1><p class="st-meta">1750 CE – 1803 CE · capital Zwiefalten</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Benedictine abbey on the Swabian Jura that bought its freedom from Württemberg and built one of Germany's great baroque churches.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zwiefalten">Zwiefalten</a> was founded in 1089 by the counts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achalm">Achalm</a> as a Benedictine monastery, settled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hirsau-abbey">Hirsau</a>. For centuries the dukes of Württemberg held its advocacy and pressed claims over it, until the abbey bought its independence in 1750 and became an imperial abbey.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The abbot ruled several villages as a prelate of the Empire and sat on the Swabian bench of prelates. Wealth from estates paid for the rebuilding of the abbey church between 1739 and 1765 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johann-michael-fischer">Johann Michael Fischer</a>, with stucco by Johann Michael Feichtmayr and frescoes by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franz-joseph-spiegler">Franz Joseph Spiegler</a>, a masterpiece of the late baroque.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1802 and 1803 the abbey was secularised and its lands given to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-wurttemberg">Duchy of Württemberg</a>. The monastery buildings became a psychiatric hospital in 1812, a role they still fill; the church is now a parish church.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/imperial-abbey-of-zwiefalten.jpg" alt="Imperial Abbey of Zwiefalten" width="320" height="270"><figcaption>Image: Franz Joseph Spiegler / Johann Michael Feuchtmayer · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Zwiefalten_Muenster.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1750 CE – 1803 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>prince bishopric</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>theocratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Zwiefalten</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Zwiefalten Abbey</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1776&amp;focus=imperial-abbey-of-zwiefalten" aria-label="Imperial Abbey of Zwiefalten on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4900 490 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Imperial Abbey of Zwiefalten lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5220 746L5229 694M5220 746L5212 730"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5229 694h0M5212 730h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5220 746h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5220 746h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1776&amp;focus=imperial-abbey-of-zwiefalten">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
